## Founding & History
Westella Vineyard is a family-owned vineyard located in Rowella, in the northern reaches of the Tamar Valley, Tasmania. The vineyard was planted in 2015 on a former cow-grazing paddock, just a few hundred meters from the kanamaluka/Tamar River. The Adkins family, Willy and Jacquie Adkins, established Westella Vineyard with a singular focus on growing Pinot Noir. They had been considering wine-growing for a long time and decided that only Pinot Noir in Tasmania was their clear answer. The first wines under the Westella label were released in 2018. The cellar door opened in 2023.

## People
Westella Vineyard is owned and operated by the Adkins family. Willy and Jacquie Adkins are the founders and winegrowers. Willy Adkins is also known for his previous role as the general manager of Tamar Ridge, a prominent Tasmanian wine brand owned by Brown Brothers. Jacquie Adkins can be reached at 0438 088 564, and Willy Adkins at 0498 262 441.

## Vineyard & Site
Westella Vineyard encompasses 10 acres (approximately 4.05 hectares) planted exclusively with Pinot Noir grapes. The vineyard is situated in Rowella, in the cool-climate Tamar Valley, near the northern end of the kanamaluka (Tamar River). This proximity to the river helps minimize frost risk while maintaining cool temperatures suitable for ripening Pinot Noir. Four different Pinot Noir clones are planted: MV6, D4V2, 115, and 777, each contributing distinct characteristics to the wines. The vines are hand-tended throughout the year, from winter pruning to hand harvesting in autumn.

## Cellar Door Experience
The cellar door at Westella Vineyard offers an intimate tasting experience. It is open Thursday to Sunday from 11:00 am to 5:00 pm, and Monday to Wednesday by appointment only. Visitors can sample and purchase wines, often accompanied by a small menu of Tasmanian produce. Advance bookings are recommended for tastings to ensure a personalized visit. The cellar door is constructed from Tasmanian Oak and offers picturesque views of the garden of vines. It is pet-friendly. For larger groups or those preferring a private space, the "Westella Tractor Room" is available, a conservatory-style room with a woodfire, LPs, and CDs, comfortably seating 12 people at a shared table.

## Food & Hospitality
Westella Vineyard offers a small menu of Tasmanian produce to accompany wine tastings, including cheese, smoked meats, condiments, and fresh produce. Visitors can enjoy curated tasting plates, such as a Cheese or Pruners Plate, showcasing locally sourced Tasmanian produce. A "Pinot and cheese" offering, described as Tasmania's unofficial state dish, features baked cheese wrapped in local smoked meats, served with sourdough, apple, pear, and Tasmanian chocolate, and is intended for two people. The "Westella Sunday School" events also include a board of Tasmanian cheeses to share. The Westella Tractor Room, available for groups, provides food from an all-Tasmanian menu with a minimum spend per head.

## Awards & Recognition
Westella Vineyard's cellar door has been described as "award-winning". In 2023, Westella Vineyard was awarded the "VinØ Program Most Improved Producer" by Wine Tasmania, recognizing their exemplary sustainable practices. The Halliday Wine Companion has rated some of Westella's wines, with the Westella Vintage Rosé 2021 Sparkling and Westella Pinot Rosé 2025 both receiving 93 silver points.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Westella Vineyard participates in VinØ, Wine Tasmania's program designed to help grape growers produce high-quality grapes and wine without negative impacts on the environment or local community. The VinØ program covers aspects such as soil health, biodiversity, carbon emissions, waste and water management, biosecurity, and social and human resources. Westella Vineyard was recognized as the "2023 VinØ Program Most Improved Producer". The vineyard focuses on managing pests and diseases responsibly, promoting biodiversity, using water efficiently, and recognizing its role in biosecurity for Tasmania's vineyards. The vines are hand-tended. The cellar door has Quality Tourism Accreditation and Sustainable Tourism Accreditation by ATIC.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Westella Vineyard is 114 Westwoods Road, Rowella, Tasmania, Australia 7270.
The phone number for Jacquie Adkins is 0438 088 564, and for Willy Adkins is 0498 262 441.
The website is westellavineyard.com.au.
The cellar door is open Thursday to Sunday from 11:00 am to 5:00 pm, and Monday to Wednesday by appointment only. It is closed in July, on Christmas Day, Boxing Day, and Good Friday.
Westella Vineyard is wheelchair accessible and has accessible bathrooms. It is pet-friendly. The vineyard participates in the Tasmanian Mixed Dozen freight scheme, allowing visitors to collect wines from various vineyards and have them shipped home.

## Notable / Distinctive
Westella Vineyard is distinctive for its singular focus on growing and producing wines exclusively from Pinot Noir grapes. This dedication to a single varietal, using four different Pinot Noir clones, allows them to craft a full range of wines, including traditional method sparklings, white Pinot Noir, rosé, and barrel-aged still Pinots. The cellar door building itself is notable, constructed from Tasmanian Oak milled locally, designed to blend with the landscape and offer an intimate, refined atmosphere. The winery also features artwork by celebrated Tasmanian artist Sue Lovegrove on its wine labels, specifically "A Long Walk, Skullbone Plains, Tasmania" (2014), reflecting the owners' passion for Tasmania's natural environment and creative arts. The vineyard actively participates in Wine Tasmania's VinØ sustainability program and was recognized as the "Most Improved Producer" in 2023. They also offer "Westella Sunday School" events for in-depth Pinot Noir exploration.

